how do I charge my clients I get this  question a lot from my readers and it's  really important when you're starting a  service-based business you have to  figure out how much you're gonna charge  and how you're gonna actually get paid  by your clients a lot of the question  again came in from a mal one of my  facebook readers he wrote in hi Evan as  a consultant how do I charge my clients  is it an hourly rate per project  percentage of the total project cost  thank you for this question I'd really  look at what phase you're at in your  business okay let me let me explain for  the customers they want cost certainty  okay this the biggest fear for a  customer is that you're gonna do an  hourly project you're gonna run way over  your time and they're gonna be way over  budget on the flip side the challenge  for you is if you do a fixed fee then  you may not be able to factor in all of  the costs that are gonna come right you  may end up working for minimum wage  because you've done so much extra work  above and beyond what you thought the  project was gonna entail my preferred  model if you're just starting up if you  are just in the startup phase if you are  not at the point of survival yet you're  not at the point where you're making  enough money to pay your bills put food  on the table we'll have to worry about  going in and get another job right if  you're not there yet  then my preferred option would be to do  a fixed fee rate so whatever the  projects gonna be you estimate how long  it's going to take you and you give your  clients certainty the reason why is  important is because you want to build a  relationship with your clients and it's  easier to get them to say yes to a deal  when they have certainty the more risks  you take away from them the easier it is  to say yes so do a fixed fee say it's  gonna cost you $2,000 $100 10,000  whatever it is you make an estimate  you're gonna be wrong but you're gonna  try to be close it'll teach you to make  your estimates better and as you develop  in your business you'll get better and  better at making estimates and the one  thing I would put in there is really  defining what the project is gonna be  about because almost always the project  changes the longer the project  for there are things that will change in  what they want you to do some things  will now become important that weren't  in there in the beginning  some things that they wanted before now  aren't important anymore they're gonna  throw away some of the project it always  happens and that's a big risk on your  part as being a consultant so you wanna  you want to watch out for project scope  creep and that's where your clients are  changing the deals on you anytime  something comes up where they want to  change something then you have to make a  decision and talk to the client and let  them know okay if we're gonna do this it  wasn't part of our initial deal but if  you want to do this then it's either  them a costume or or we have to take  something out and keeping those lines of  communication open always because it  builds a relationship with you in the  client and it also prevents bad feelings  you know if the client keeps saying add  this add this add this and suddenly  you're working so many extra hours are  not able to deliver it on a budget that  you're happy with then it's not going to  be a good relationship with you and the  client and it's gonna probably end up  and in some kind of fight and the  project not finish it so be conscious of  that be consider the project scope creep  but at the start to make it easy for  your clients I would say start on a  fixed fee base so they have cost  certainty okay this is only to get to  the phase of survival to get to the  point where you're making enough money  that you don't have to worry about going  back into the job I'm not talking about  living a life of luxury here this is  basics the challenge of the service  based business is it's hard to scale  it's hard to really scale up a service  because you're always trading your time  for money  and your time is limited you could bring  on other people to work under you that's  the best way to do it if you need to  scale but it's still so much harder to  scale a service based business than a  product if you look at the businesses  that venture capitals invest in if you  look at the businesses that are throwing  off huge returns they're not service  based companies because it's too hard to  scale so if you want to have a major  impact if you want to make a lot more  money if you want to really reach your  community then you want to start  thinking about how you  can either as a consultant take a piece  of the company that you're working with  or start to productize your knowledge  and information because that's going to  help you have that bigger impact okay  but I don't want you to do that from the  beginning because that takes more time  is riskier and you're gonna run out of  money before those things come through  most likely so at the start get to know  your clients more closely with them do  fixed fee projects get to the point of  survival to the point where you are not  worried about having to go back and get  another job and then from there you can  afford small risks and thinking about  taking a piece of the companies you're  working with starting to productize your  information and starting to have a  bigger impact that will help you scale  up your knowledge and expertise believe  those you watching liked the video  please give it a thumbs up below it  makes me to do more for you and I'd love  to hear what you have to think leave a  comment under this video and I will make  a video response if you have a question  thank you so much guys and I'll see you  soon
 

